#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be5f2f80-fd83-5eed-b8b4-871bddf6ded1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352316} {:answer/id #uuid "65013fd6-99f6-5f4e-8d39-ac190858fc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352317} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e99dc8-ae1d-5df7-81bc-d75b625d59aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352318} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb48cfe-0299-5b9e-b426-4a7d12119287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352319} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d44521-7e93-5fe2-a4b1-57e13e30d769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-7,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb48cfe-0299-5b9e-b426-4a7d12119287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352319} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb3be6f4-6b46-5198-adc6-f1445f5f5f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6db048bd-fcb3-5632-92a9-6961f0b5074a" :question/math-academy-id "q-116189" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x-35) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x-5).$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-7$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7346db0-9dcd-5a2c-aea2-9aa9583e57e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "e27f4f2a-b397-5038-bb43-2808e6bed039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352324} {:answer/id #uuid "4529660d-37f1-5916-af96-0b059d0c5a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352325} {:answer/id #uuid "01de2e5e-a5ac-5c6d-9ed9-bbe6a164b2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352326} {:answer/id #uuid "fb615cdf-4492-50de-9643-ff4c1ed02f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27f4f2a-b397-5038-bb43-2808e6bed039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352324} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da1890b-ab4c-5e5d-ac30-9808c228357a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d79bf4af-b3e2-564d-8963-3b028b0571ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-116194" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x & \\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x-4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+4)(2x-1) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+4)(2x-1).$ The roots are $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+4)(2x-1)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+4)(2x-1)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+4)(2x-1)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c34be5fa-342f-55f1-8010-fe2a311588d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c34be5fa-342f-55f1-8010-fe2a311588d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352330} :answer-field/id #uuid "f44dcd68-0fd8-5f38-9193-5b51a293ada4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86413ed8-599d-5b1f-96ef-819d6b08d1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86413ed8-599d-5b1f-96ef-819d6b08d1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352332} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5e474d-9c2e-5a9b-a21b-3c56587f6e5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58fd3bd3-662f-5eb8-98ba-b1ef6da33193" :question/math-academy-id "q-277163" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+x-30<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-30 & <0 \\\\ (x+6)(x-5) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x-5)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x-5)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+x-30<0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-6,5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71550b14-774c-5535-8d1e-41ee55757918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71550b14-774c-5535-8d1e-41ee55757918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352335} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f5fa4f-4f72-5d73-84d5-210e7fd8eafe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baf33220-23fb-512a-9d06-4d3a50d79865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baf33220-23fb-512a-9d06-4d3a50d79865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352337} :answer-field/id #uuid "12abe58f-42b4-5a13-8e4e-37af2c99f4fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de8f525f-9eb7-5b74-8764-f14d21d4fb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278314" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-2x-3<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-3 & <0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+1)(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,3),$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+1)(x-3)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+1)(x-3)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+1)(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-2x-3<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e1c02b6-93a2-5778-be32-f59c7ad2737d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-5, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052939} {:answer/id #uuid "aeef4d61-6aed-5d74-9de0-5281f4107344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-5)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052940} {:answer/id #uuid "ef79a70c-ab79-5a84-b6c0-c6cf41b359b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞, 0)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052941} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd68642-f535-53b0-97f8-194ae755d2b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (0, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052942} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3fdc40-a76f-5ecb-bf32-24ad6a2c1c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-5)∪(0, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef79a70c-ab79-5a84-b6c0-c6cf41b359b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞, 0)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052941} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa86cc39-c01f-55aa-a707-73e166941c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43671ef1-4b81-53b4-8687-766126e69d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-44625" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-5x>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x & >0 \\\\ x(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ \nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-5x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$"}]}
